# Network Models
      OSI Model (7 Layers): Conceptual model to understand network communication.

# OSI Model (Open Systems Interconnection Model)
  The OSI Model is a conceptual framework that standardizes the functions of a telecommunication or computing system into 7 abstraction layers. 
  
  It helps in understanding how data travels from one device to another over a network.

🚧 PURPOSE OF OSI MODEL:
> Defines how network communication should occur.
> Helps troubleshoot network issues by isolating layers.
> Promotes interoperability between different systems and vendors.

🎯 Why is OSI Model Important?
   - Helps design networks clearly.

>Supports troubleshooting by isolating issues to a specific layer.
>Encourages standardization across hardware and software vendors.
>Helps in certifications (like CCNA, Network+).



🧱 The 7 Layers of OSI Model (Bottom to Top):

1️⃣ Physical Layer (Layer 1) – "Hardware Layer"

> Deals with the physical medium for transmitting bits (0s and 1s).
> Includes cables, voltages, pins, connectors, hubs.
> No understanding of the meaning of bits.

-> Electrical signals or Wi-Fi signals sent through the network

Example: Ethernet cables, Fiber optics, USB, Bluetooth, Wi-Fi signals.

2️⃣ Data Link Layer (Layer 2) – "Node-to-node delivery"
 > Responsible for framing, MAC addressing, and error detection.

 > Ensures data transfer is error-free between two directly connected nodes.

->   Frames are formed with MAC addresses, sent over local network

💡 Example: Switches, MAC addresses, Ethernet, ARP, PPP.


3️⃣ Network Layer (Layer 3) – "Routing Layer"

 > Provides logical addressing (IP) and routing of packets across multiple networks.

 > Determines best path from source to destination.

-> Each segment gets IP addresses (sender/receiver) and routed through Internet

💡 Example: Routers, IP Address, ICMP, OSPF, RIP.

4️⃣ Transport Layer (Layer 4) – "End-to-End Delivery"
 > Ensures reliable transmission, error recovery, and flow control.

 >Two main protocols:
  TCP (Transmission Control Protocol) – reliable.
  UDP (User Datagram Protocol) – faster but not reliable.

-> Message split into TCP segments and sent.

💡 Example: TCP (used in web browsing), UDP (used in video streaming, VoIP).

5️⃣ Session Layer (Layer 5) – "Session Management"
 > Establishes, manages, and terminates sessions between two devices.
 > Synchronizes communication and manages dialog control.

-> Session between sender and recipient is managed

💡 Example: Logging into websites, Remote procedure calls (RPC), SQL sessions.

6️⃣ Presentation Layer (Layer 6) – "Translator Layer"
 > Translates data between application and network formats.

 > Handles data encryption/decryption, compression/decompression, and data conversion.

-> Message is encrypted using end-to-end encryption

💡 Example: SSL/TLS (for HTTPS), JPEG, MP3, GIF formats.

7️⃣ Application Layer (Layer 7) – "User Interface Layer"
 > Closest to the end-user.
 > Provides network services to applications like email, file transfer, and web browsing.

->   User types the message in WhatsApp app

💡 Example: HTTP (web), SMTP (email), FTP (file transfer), DNS.



# What is TCP/IP Model?
TCP/IP (Transmission Control Protocol/Internet Protocol) is a networking model that defines how data is sent, received, and communicated over the Internet and computer networks.

It provides a set of rules and protocols that allow different devices such as computers, servers, mobile phones, and routers to communicate with each other.

In simple words, TCP/IP is the language that devices use to talk to each other on a network.

Example
When you open Google, send a WhatsApp message, watch YouTube, or download a file, TCP/IP is working behind the scenes.

# Why is TCP/IP Model Used?

TCP/IP is used to establish communication between devices connected to a network.

Without TCP/IP, devices would not know how to send, receive, or understand data.

Main Reasons
To enable communication between devices.
To transfer data reliably.
To identify devices using IP addresses.
To route data from source to destination.
To support Internet communication.

Example
A Windows laptop can communicate with a Linux server because both use TCP/IP protocols.

# Where is TCP/IP Model Used?
TCP/IP is used in almost every type of network.

> Used In
Internet
Cloud Computing
Data Centers
Corporate Networks
Home Wi-Fi Networks
Mobile Networks
Example
When you access a website hosted on AWS, TCP/IP is used to transfer data between your device and the server.

# When is TCP/IP Model Used?
TCP/IP is used whenever data needs to travel from one device to another.

Situations
Browsing websites
Sending emails
Video calling
File downloading
Online gaming
Cloud services
Example
When you search "DevOps Roadmap" on Google, TCP/IP is used to send your request and receive the results.

# How Does TCP/IP Model Work?
TCP/IP works using four layers. Each layer performs a specific task before passing data to the next layer.

Step 1: Application Layer
The user interacts with an application.

Example:
You open Chrome and type "www.google.com".
The browser creates an HTTP request.

Step 2: Transport Layer
The data is divided into smaller pieces called segments.

> TCP ensures:
Reliable delivery
Error checking
Correct order of data

Example:
If a file is being downloaded and one part is lost, TCP requests that part again.

Step 3: Internet Layer
This layer adds source and destination IP addresses.
It decides the best route for the data.

Example:
Source IP:
192.168.1.10

Destination IP:
142.250.xxx.xxx (Google Server)

The router uses these IP addresses to forward packets.

Step 4: Network Access Layer
This layer sends data through the physical network.
Data travels through:
Wi-Fi
Ethernet Cable
Fiber Optic Cable

The data is converted into signals and transmitted.

# 1. Application Layer
Creates a request for the YouTube webpage.

2. Transport Layer
Breaks the request into segments and ensures reliability using TCP.

3. Internet Layer
Adds IP addresses and finds the route to the YouTube server.

4. Network Access Layer
Sends the data through Wi-Fi or cable.

Result
The YouTube server receives the request and sends the webpage back using the same process in reverse order.


# Advantages of TCP/IP Model
Standard networking model.
Used worldwide on the Internet.
Reliable communication.
Supports different operating systems.
Scalable and flexible.
Allows communication between different types of devices.

# Key Points
TCP/IP is the foundation of the Internet.
It contains 4 layers.
TCP provides reliable communication.
IP provides addressing and routing.
Every website, cloud service, and online application uses TCP/IP.